{:edb/db-before-t 2308 :edb/db-after-t 2309 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535621 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:49:29.698Z" 13194139535621 true] [17592186119488 1100 "Consider the curve  $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 2t-1 ,\\: 8t+2 ,\\: -2t \\right\\rangle, \\, t \\geq 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $\\mathbf{r}(0)$ to $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $s=6\\sqrt{2}t.$ Find $\\mathbf R(s),$ the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s.$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186119488 1100 "Consider the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 2t-1,\\: 8t+2,\\: -2t \\right\\rangle, \\, t \\geq 0$, for which the arc length $s$ from $\\mathbf{r}(0)$ to $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $s=6\\sqrt{2}t$. Find $\\mathbf R(s)$, the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s$." 13194139535621 false] [17592186119488 1145 "First, since\n\n$$\ns=6\\sqrt{2}t\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6\\sqrt{2}t &= s \\\\\nt &= \\frac{s}{6\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\nt &= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length $s$, with\n\n$$\ns \\geq 0\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(s) &= r(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12}) \\\\\n&= ⟨2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12}) - 1, 8(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12}) + 2,-2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{6} - 1, \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}s}{6} + 2,-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{6}⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}⟨\\sqrt{2}s - 6, 4\\sqrt{2}s + 12,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535621 false] [17592186119488 1145 "First, since  $s=6\\sqrt{2}t,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{2}t & =s \\\\ t & =\\frac{s}{6\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length $s,$ with $s \\geq 0,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12}) \\\\  & =⟨2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12})-1,\\,8(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12})+2,\\,-2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{6}-1,\\,\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}s}{6}+2,\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{6}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\sqrt{2}s-6,\\,4\\sqrt{2}s+12,\\,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186119489 1100 "What is the parametrization of the curve  $\\mathbf r(t)=2(t\\cos{t}-\\sin{t})\\,\\mathbf i+2(t\\sin{t}+\\cos{t})\\,\\mathbf j, \\, t \\geq 0$ by its arc length $s?$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186119489 1100 "What is the parametrization of the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=2(t\\cos{t}-\\sin{t})\\,\\mathbf i+2(t\\sin{t}+\\cos{t})\\,\\mathbf j, \\, t \\geq 0$ by its arc length $s$?" 13194139535621 false] [17592186119489 1145 "First, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and its magnitude $\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= 2\\frac{d}{dt}(t\\cos t - \\sin t)i + 2\\frac{d}{dt}(t\\sin t + \\cos t)j \\\\\n&= 2(\\cos t - t\\sin t - \\cos t)i + 2(\\sin t + t\\cos t - \\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2t\\sin ti + 2t\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\mid 2t \\mid \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we removed the absolute value bars since $t>0$.\n\nNext, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ∫_{0}^{t}‖r^{′}(u)‖du \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{t}2udu \\\\\n&= u^{2} \\mid _{0}^{t} \\\\\n&= t^{2} - 0 \\\\\n&= t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $s=t^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= s \\\\\nt &= ± \\sqrt{s} \\\\\nt &= \\sqrt{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ntaking the positive root because $t>0$.\n\nFinally, the vector function that parametrizes $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(s) &= r(\\sqrt{s}) \\\\\n&= 2(\\sqrt{s}\\cos \\sqrt{s} - \\sin \\sqrt{s})i + 2(\\sqrt{s}\\sin \\sqrt{s} + \\cos \\sqrt{s})j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535621 false] [17592186119489 1145 "First, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and its magnitude $\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|2t|\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we removed the absolute value bars since $t>0.$ \n\nNext, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}2u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $s=t^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =s \\\\ t & =\\pm \\sqrt{s} \\\\ t & =\\sqrt{s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ntaking the positive root because $t>0.$  \n\nFinally, the vector function that parametrizes $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt{s}) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s})\\,i+2(\\sqrt{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186119490 1100 "Given the curve  $\\mathbf r(t)=\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf i - \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf j+\\sqrt{3}t\\,\\mathbf k, \\, t \\geq 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by $s=2t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $\\mathbf{T}(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186119490 1100 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf i - \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf j+\\sqrt{3}t\\,\\mathbf k, \\, t \\geq 0$, for which the arc length $s$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by $s=2t$, find the corresponding unit tangent $\\mathbf{T}(s)$, parametrized by $s$." 13194139535621 false] [17592186119490 1145 "First, since $s=2t$, we have that\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{s}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $\\mathbf R(s)$ that parametrizes $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(s) &= r(\\frac{s}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\cos (\\frac{s}{2})i - \\sin (\\frac{s}{2})j + \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{s}{2}k \\\\\n&= \\cos (\\frac{s}{2})i - \\sin (\\frac{s}{2})j + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}s}{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(s) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}s}\\mathbf R(s)\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(s) &= \\frac{d}{ds}R(s) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{ds}(\\cos (\\frac{s}{2}))i + \\frac{d}{ds}(-\\sin (\\frac{s}{2}))j + \\frac{d}{ds}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}s}{2})k \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (\\frac{s}{2})i - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (\\frac{s}{2})j + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(-\\sin (\\frac{s}{2})i - \\cos (\\frac{s}{2})j + \\sqrt{3}k)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535621 false] [17592186119490 1145 "First, since $s=2t,$ we have that $t=\\dfrac{s}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $\\mathbf R(s)$ that parametrizes $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{s}{2}k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}s}{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s,$ the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(s) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}s}\\mathbf R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}s}{2})k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186119669 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357849 13194139535621 true] [17592186119669 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357856 13194139535621 true] [17592186119669 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357891 13194139535621 true] [17592186119669 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357905 13194139535621 true] [17592186119670 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357870 13194139535621 true] [17592186119670 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357877 13194139535621 true] [17592186119670 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357884 13194139535621 true] [17592186119670 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357912 13194139535621 true] [17592186119670 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357919 13194139535621 true] [17592186119671 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357863 13194139535621 true] [17592186119671 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357898 13194139535621 true] [17592186119671 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357926 13194139535621 true] [17592186119671 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357933 13194139535621 true] [17592186120212 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535621 true] [17592186120212 1100 "Consider the curve $r(t) = ⟨2t, 1 - 3t, 5 + 6t⟩$, $t \\ge 0$, for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s = 7t$. Find $R(s)$, the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s$." 13194139535621 false] [17592186120212 1100 "Consider the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,1-3t,\\,5+6t⟩$, $t\\ge 0$, for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=7t.$ Find $R(s),$ the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s.$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186120212 1145 "First, since $s=7t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{7}.$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s$, with $s\\ge 0$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{7}) \\\\  & =⟨2(\\frac{s}{7}),\\,1-3(\\frac{s}{7}),\\,5+6(\\frac{s}{7})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}⟨2s,\\,7-3s,\\,35+6s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186120213 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535621 true] [17592186120213 1100 "Given the curve  $r(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+6t\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=10t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186120213 1100 "Given the curve $r(t) = 8\\cos ti + 8\\sin tj + 6tk$, $t \\ge 0$, for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s = 10t$, find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s)$, parametrized by $s$." 13194139535621 false] [17592186120213 1145 "First, since $s=10t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{10}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+6(\\frac{s}{10})k \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3s}{5}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{s}{10})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s}{5})k \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186357849 1017 #edb/ref 17592186357850 13194139535621 true] [17592186357849 1089 "q-127687" 13194139535621 true] [17592186357849 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535621 true] [17592186357849 1100 "Consider the curve $r(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s={e}^{t}-1.$ Given that $R(s)$ is the parametrization of the given curve by its arc length $s,$ what is the $x$-component of $R?$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186357849 1113 #uuid "1ce59150-0075-56ed-b6d6-42ed469b42d7" 13194139535621 true] [17592186357849 1145 "First, since $s={e}^{t}-1,$ we have that $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1).$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s,$ with $s\\ge 0,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(s+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(s+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the $x$-component of $R$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(s+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)].\n$$" 13194139535621 true] [17592186357850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357851 13194139535621 true] [17592186357850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357852 13194139535621 true] [17592186357850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357853 13194139535621 true] [17592186357850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357854 13194139535621 true] [17592186357850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357855 13194139535621 true] [17592186357850 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535621 true] [17592186357850 1041 "selection" 13194139535621 true] [17592186357850 1104 #edb/ref 17592186357852 13194139535621 true] [17592186357850 1110 #uuid "d99044ff-d933-5a1f-ac00-00762d7e9a75" 13194139535621 true] [17592186357851 1034 "s\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]" 13194139535621 true] [17592186357851 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535621 true] [17592186357851 1111 #uuid "a8dbe34d-2147-5006-bfe6-f3c9a8614546" 13194139535621 true] [1759218635785